/-!
# Weight-2m criterion on Γ₁(M): X·(vartheta j)^m is a q-expansion

Theorem statement:
`ModularCurve.exists_modularForm_gamma1_qExpansion_eq_mul_thetaL_pow_of_isIntegral`
(`1c7c4305-3382-599b-8a46-794fc3fd644d`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `4702dada-d30b-5bc6-962d-19a441b8573c`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Fix $M \geq 1$ (non-zero) and an integer $m \geq 1$, and let $X$ be a Laurent series over
$\mathbb{C}$. Assume $X$ lies in [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange ℂ (ModularCurve.qExpFunctionFieldC
ℚ (CongruenceSubgroup.Gamma1 M))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), that is, in the
subfield of $\mathbb{C}((q))$ generated over $\mathbb{C}$ by the coefficientwise images under
$\mathbb{Q} \to \mathbb{C}$ of the subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the
quotients $p_f/p_g$ of integral $q$-expansions of pairs of modular forms $f,g$ of one and the same
weight on $\Gamma_1(M)$ with $p_g \neq 0$. Write $\bar{j} =$ [`ModularCurve.jqModC
ℂ`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L15) for the Laurent series $q^{-1}\,(E_4^3 \cdot \eta\text{-unit
inverse})$, the $q$-expansion of the $j$-invariant. Assume further that
$X^6\,\bar{j}^{4m}\,(\bar{j}-1728)^{3m}$ is integral over the $\mathbb{C}$-subalgebra generated by
$\bar{j}$, and that $X^2\,\bar{j}^{m}\,(\bar{j}-1728)^{m}$ is integral over the
$\mathbb{C}$-subalgebra generated by $\bar{j}^{-1}$. Then there exists a modular form $f$ of weight
$2m$ for $\Gamma_1(M)$ whose $q$-expansion of width $1$, viewed as a Laurent series, equals $X \cdot
(\vartheta\bar{j})^m$, where $\vartheta$ is the operator
[`ModularCurve.thetaL`](def/ModularCurve_QExpansionDiff.html#L16) sending a Laurent series $w$ to
$q\,\frac{d}{dq}w$.

This is the $\Gamma_1(M)$ form of the classical criterion recognising a $q$-series as the expansion
of a holomorphic modular form: the first integrality hypothesis controls the analytic order of
vanishing on the upper half-plane, the second the growth at every cusp, and the form is obtained by
dividing modular forms. It is used to produce enough forms of weight $2m$ on $\Gamma_1(M)$ for the
dimension lower bounds
[`ModularForm.exists_linearIndependent_gamma1_dimFormula_le_card_of_even`]
(thm.html#ModularForm.exists_linearIndependent_gamma1_dimFormula_le_card_of_even)
and
[`ModularForm.exists_linearIndependent_gamma1_dimFormula_le_card_of_odd`]
(thm.html#ModularForm.exists_linearIndependent_gamma1_dimFormula_le_card_of_odd).

